t of halving headroom for the top legitimate text. Trade-off: given FN cost >> FP cost (FN = machine signs a human-judgment doc; FP = extra human call), 0.686 is arguably better than 0.697. Hmm — but wait, is that right? The fence only escalates; false positive is just a human call. The system's stated purpose: ensure human judgment where needed. A false negative is a machine signing an \"absolution\"-like document — potentially legal damage. A false positive is friction. So asymmetric loss favors lower threshold... but only until FPs become so common that humans rubber-stamp (alarm fatigue), which converts FPs into effective FNs. That's the real systemic risk: 0 annoyances on 5 ordinary closings is a 0/5 sample — 95% CI upper bound on FP rate ~45% (rule of three: 3/5=60%? Actually rule of three: 3/n = 3/5 = 60% upper bound at 95%). So we know almost nothing about FP rate.\n   - So attack 2: the validation is circular by construction; the measured \"9/9, 0/5\" is guaranteed a priori; the only non-circular datum is the 2 declared FPs and the margin width. Measurement: frozen-threshold held-out test with pre-registered pass criteria (e.g., ≥N evasions from a new attacker who knows the threshold — assume attacker knows it, kerckhoffs), and FP rate on a sample of real production closings big enough to bound the rate (e.g., 200 texts → CI ±~3%).\n\n3. The 0.015/0.015 knife edge on unseen texts: the nearest legitimate (0.671) and nearest evasion (0.701) both sit 0.015 from threshold. But those are just the nearest observed; the true overlap is unknown. With unseen text, an attacker explicitly briefed to evade got to 0.701 — that's the attacker's WORST attempt of 9, and the attacker didn't know the threshold (or did they? \"con el encargo explícito de evadir el cerco\" — evade fence A presumably at 0.70 on model A? unclear which fence they attacked). Key question: did the attacker know model B would be added and its threshold? If the attacker optimized against fence A only