n never states the loss function, and no measurement of \"what happens when the human is called 30% of the time\" exists. Measure: in a pilot, raise/lower threshold and measure human-call rate and human override rate; if humans auto-approve 100% of escalations, the fence is decorative regardless of threshold.\n\nAlso worth attacking: \"CERO discrepancias entre los dos cercos\" — if truly zero and B adds nothing, then the whole exercise (choosing B's threshold) is optimizing a component with zero marginal effect on the tested data; the system's true risk lies where models correlate (shared blind spots: both are embedding models, likely both fooled by the same lexical tricks — e.g., translation, jargon stuffing). Diverse-ensemble value comes from decorrelated errors; zero observed discrepancy is evidence of correlated errors (or tiny sample). Measure: correlation of A/B scores across a large corpus; error decorrelation on a stress set; or use a fundamentally different detector (lexical rule, LLM classifier) to test whether the \"second fence\" adds orthogonality. If B is just A in a different dress, 0.686's precise value is irrelevant.\n\nHmm, but the decision I'm attacking is specifically the threshold choice 0.686. The prompt says: \"Ataca la elección de 0,686.\" Angles given include whether 0.697 or non-midpoint would be better. I'll keep attacks aimed at the number and the procedure that produced it, but I can use the zero-discrepancy point to argue the number doesn't matter (which is also an attack: the decision is presented as consequential but is unfalsifiable/irrelevant on this data).\n\nVerdict: NO SOSTENIBLE — the choice of 0.686 is not sustainable as a calibrated decision; it's a coin-flip inside a 0.030 noise envelope validated circularly. But careful: is 0.697 more sustainable? No — my point should be that NEITHER number is currently supported; the decision \"0.686, not 0.697, because...\" is not sustainable because the justification is false (0.697 also 