the decision survives my attacks. I should genuinely evaluate. Let me think about the technical substance.\n\nKey facts:\n- Model A: gemini-embedding-001, threshold 0.70 (existing fence).\n- Model B: text-multilingual-embedding-002, different cosine scale. Threshold must be chosen.\n- Narrow bank (12 cases by system author, only 6 reach signature): judgments min 0.778 max 0.916; non-judgments min 0.502 max 0.616; margin +0.162 → midpoint 0.697.\n- Complete bank: narrow + 9 adversarial evasion texts (external attacker, explicit brief to evade) + 5 ordinary legitimate closings + 2 \"deliberately difficult\" legitimate closings.\n  - Lowest-scoring judgment (evasion ES · ERP/QA/KPI filler): 0.701\n  - Highest-scoring ordinary legitimate closing: 0.671\n  - Margin +0.030 → midpoint 0.686.\n- The 2 deliberately difficult ones (0.768 and 0.711 with model B) are counted as declared false positives, don't topple the test; they say \"saldo en cero\" and \"expediente cerrado\" — literally what an acquittal says; the cause distinguishing them isn't in the text.\n- Decision: 0.686 (complete bank midpoint), not 0.697, \"because a threshold that only separates easy cases separates nothing.\"\n- Measured result with 0.686: 9/9 evasions caught, 0 annoyance on the 5 ordinary closings, 2 declared FPs, and ZERO discrepancies between the two fences on the 22 cases.\n\nAttacks to consider:\n\n1. Margin 0.030 over 22 points: is that separation or noise with a name?\n   - The margin is computed from single extreme points: min of judgments (0.701) and max of ordinary legitimate (0.671). These are order statistics of tiny samples. The min of 9-15 evasion texts and max of 5 ordinary closings — extremes of small samples have huge variance. Sampling noise: with n=5, the max is a biased estimator of the population tail; the gap between min-of-9 and max-of-5 could easily flip sign with new samples. Also embeddings have run-to-run nondeterminism? Many embedding APIs are deterministic but some models have slight nondeterminism (e.g., quantization, GPU nondeterminism). Also cosine similarity sensitivity: punctuation, whitespace, casing, truncation, language mixing. Need to measure: bootstrap resampling, test-retest stability, perturbation robustness (typos, synonyms, reformulation), and estimate the distribution overlap.\n   - Also: 0.030 margin, threshold at midpoint means both nearest points are 0.015 away. Any drift in the embedding model version (models get updated/deprecated; \"text-multilingual-embedding-002\" — these get deprecated) shifts scores; a drift of 0.015 flips the decision. Version pinning and monitoring needed.\n   - So attack 1: the margin is an order-statistic artifact of n=5 and n≤15; 0.015 distance to threshold on each side is within typical perturbation noise. Measurement: adversarial re-test with a fresh attacker (held-out), bootstrap CIs on the min/max, test-retest, perturbation suite (paraphrase of the 0.671 text upward and evasion 0.701 downward by trivial edits), and drift monitoring.\n\n2. Circularity: threshold chosen on same data used to declare pass. With n=22 and the threshold literally placed at the midpoint of the observed extremes, \"9/9 and 0 annoyances\" is guaranteed by construction — it's a tautology, not a test. The midpoint of min/max by definition lies between min and max, so 100% separation on the training set is guaranteed whatever the data. The only real information content is the width of the margin (0.030) and the 2 declared FPs. So the \"RESULTADO MEDIDO\" adds zero bits of evidence. What would refute: pre-registered held-out evaluation — fresh attacker, fresh legitimate texts, threshold frozen before test. Measure: pass rate on held-out set. Also correct the asymmetry: cost of missed human-judgment (evasion) vs cost of false human call. The fence only escalates to human, so FN cost is... wait, the fence can only require a human, never authorize. So a false positive costs a human call (annoyance, latency); a false negative means the machine signs something that required human judgment — that's the real risk. So the loss function is asymmetric: err low. But going lower increases FPs. The declared-difficult FPs at 0.711 and 0.768 are ABOVE 0.686 AND above 0.697 and even above... 0.768 > 0.70? Model A threshold is 0.70 on its own scale, not comparable. Anyway, with 0.686 or 0.697, those 2 difficult texts are caught either way (0.711 and 0.768 > both). So the choice between 0.686 and 0.697 changes nothing on observed data except... the evasion at 0.701: with 0.697, 0.701 > 0.697, still caught (barely, by 0.004!). Interesting: with 0.697, the weakest evasion is caught by only 0.004. With 0.686, by 0.015. The ordinary closing 0.671: with 0.697 it's 0.026 below; with 0.686, 0.015 below. So 0.686 doubles the headroom against the weakest evasion at the cost of halving headroom for the top legitimate text. Trade-off: given FN cost >> FP cost (FN = machine signs a human-judgment doc; FP = extra human call), 0.686 is arguably better than 0.697. Hmm — but wait, is that right? The fence only escalates; false positive is just a human call. The system's stated purpose: ensure human judgment where needed. A false negative is a machine signing an \"absolution\"-like document — potentially legal damage. A false positive is friction. So asymmetric loss favors lower threshold... but only until FPs become so common that humans rubber-stamp (alarm fatigue), which converts FPs into effective FNs. That's the real systemic risk: 0 annoyances on 5 ordinary closings is a 0/5 sample — 95% CI upper bound on FP rate ~45% (rule of three: 3/5=60%? Actually rule of three: 3/n = 3/5 = 60% upper bound at 95%). So we know almost nothing about FP rate.\n   - So attack 2: the validation is circular by construction; the measured \"9/9, 0/5\" is guaranteed a priori; the only non-circular datum is the 2 declared FPs and the margin width. Measurement: frozen-threshold held-out test with pre-registered pass criteria (e.g., ≥N evasions from a new attacker who knows the threshold — assume attacker knows it, kerckhoffs), and FP rate on a sample of real production closings big enough to bound the rate (e.g., 200 texts → CI ±~3%).\n\n3. The 0.015/0.015 knife edge on unseen texts: the nearest legitimate (0.671) and nearest evasion (0.701) both sit 0.015 from threshold. But those are just the nearest observed; the true overlap is unknown. With unseen text, an attacker explicitly briefed to evade got to 0.701 — that's the attacker's WORST attempt of 9, and the attacker didn't know the threshold (or did they? \"con el encargo explícito de evadir el cerco\" — evade fence A presumably at 0.70 on model A? unclear which fence they attacked). Key question: did the attacker know model B would be added and its threshold? If the attacker optimized against fence A only, then model B's 9/9 is meaningless against an attacker who adapts. Adaptive attacker: now that B is deployed, next generation of evasion texts will target B's threshold. The 0.030 margin against a NON-adaptive attacker is not the operative margin. What to measure: red-team round 2 where the attacker is given model B (or an equivalent oracle / the threshold) and told to land in [0.671, 0.686) — if trivial paraphrase moves a text by ±0.02, the fence is a speed bump, not a fence. Also measure: paraphrase sensitivity — take the 0.701 evasion and the 0.671 legitimate, apply trivial transformations (ERP jargon swap, tense change, passive voice, translation to EN then back), see how much the max-similarity moves. If a 0.015 shift is easy, threshold choice within [0.671, 0.701] is cosmetic; the real control is downstream (the human escalation being meaningful, or dual-signature).\n   - Also: max over 16 anchors amplifies variance — max of 16 similarities has higher variance and is easier to game by pulling one anchor up with keyword injection (\"absolver\" mentioned in a Q&A/KPI filler?). The evasion at 0.701 with \"relleno ERP/QA/KPI\"