ainst A. The last one is the real justification: an adaptive attacker who defeats A might trip B. But that value is UNTESTED — indeed the whole test shows B redundant on non-adaptive data. To measure B's marginal value: generate texts that defeat A (score <0.70 on A's scale while being judgments) and check B's catch rate on them. That's the only experiment that justifies B's existence. If B catches few A-defeating texts, B is dead weight that only adds FP surface (16 anchors × 2 models = 32 chances for lexical false fire) and maintenance burden. Also: were the 9 evasions crafted against A or against both? If the attacker only ever saw A, the \"9/9 caught by B\" is trivial.\n   - Actually wait: \"CERO discrepancias entre los dos cercos en los 22 casos\" — zero discrepancies means on every case both fences agree (both fire or both don't). But the 2 difficult FPs at 0.768 and 0.711 with B — with A, do they also fire? If they agree, then A also fires on them. And the 5 ordinary closings: both silent. And the 9 evasions: hmm, wait. The evasions were written to evade the fence — but with 0.686 on B, all 9 are caught (score > 0.686). If A also catches all 9 (zero discrepancy), then the attacker FAILED to evade A too?? Then the 9 evasions aren't evasions of the current system at all — they only \"evade\" if they were targeted at some other configuration. Hold on — \"un atacante externo escribió con el encargo explícito de evadir el cerco\" — evade THE fence (singular, i.e., A at 0.70). If A catches all 9 too (zero discrepancies with B catching all 9), then the evader failed entirely, and the whole bank's adversarial value is questionable: the attacker was bad, or didn't know A's threshold/model. An attacker who can't defeat a single known fence provides almost no information about the margin's robustness. That's a strong attack: the \"evasiones\" that define the 0.030 margin never actually evaded anything — the lowest one at 0.701 (B scale) would also need checking on A's scale. Zero discrepancies means they all fired A as well. So the adversarial bank is adversarial in name only. The real question: what do texts that ACTUALLY evade A look like on B's scale? None exist in the bank. The margin that matters — between A-evading judgments and legitimate texts on B's scale — is unmeasured. Measurement: iterated red-teaming against the deployed pair (attacker gets feedback \"caught/not caught\" for k rounds — standard adversarial interleave), then measure B's scale on the surviving texts.\n   - That's the sharpest attack, I think: the evaluation has no surviving adversarial example, so we know nothing about the operative frontier. The 0.030 margin is between two points that both fail to matter (one that gets caught anyway by A, and... well the legitimate one matters for FP).\n\n5. Would 0.697 be better, or a non-midpoint threshold? Points: midpoint of min/max is an arbitrary estimator — maximizes minimum distance to the two observed extremes, but those extremes are noisy and the loss is asymmetric. Given FN cost (machine signs judgment doc) >> FP cost (human call) — though with alarm-fatigue caveat — the threshold should be set by loss ratio and estimated distributions, not midpoint. E.g., set threshold at legit_max + α or at a quantile of the evasion distribution with headroom: e.g., 0.686 vs 0.697 both leave weakest evasion margin of 0.015/0.004. If you truly believe evasions cluster ≥0.701, the lower you go the more headroom, but FPs rise. With only 5 ordinary closings, FP rate unknown. Non-midpoint options: (a) choose threshold = max over observed legit texts minus nothing... hmm. Actually given asymmetry, threshold below 0.671 would be defensible if FPs are cheap: e.g., 0.65 or even 0.62 (above narrow-bank non-judgment max 0.616) — that keeps headroom of 0.05+ against the weakest evasion while sacrificing... but the 2 difficult declared FPs are already above everything, so they're FPs at any threshold below 0.768; going to 0.65 only adds FPs among ordinary closings (max 0.671 → one FP). The real point: the choice 0.686 vs 0.697 is a 0.011 quibble inside a 0.030 envelope — the decision's stated rationale (\"a threshold that only separates easy cases separates nothing\") attacks a strawman: 0.697 also separates the observed complete-bank cases (0.701 > 0.697). Both thresholds pass the same data; the choice between them is unfalsifiable on this data. What would decide it: FP rate estimate at both thresholds on a large production sample + adaptive red team + explicit loss function. Alternatively: hysteresis/soft zone — scores in the gray band [0.65, 0.72] go to a cheaper human review queue anyway... but that's proposing improvement; as a dissident I should note that the binary threshold at midpoint is fragile and the decision between 0.686/0.697 is empirically empty on this data.\n   - Also worth noting: the \"declared false positives\" move is a protocol smell: declaring the 2 difficult cases as FPs-by fiat (\"no tumban la prueba\") after seeing their scores is post-hoc relabeling. If you can declare inconvenient positives as \"declared FP,\" the test can never fail — it's unfalsifiable. The justification (\"the distinguishing cause isn't in the text\") is actually reasonable (a closure saying \"saldo en cero\" is genuinely ambiguous from text alone — arguably SHOULD go to human!), but then the metric \"0 molestias sobre los 5 cierres ordinarios\" cherry-picks: the FP rate including difficult-but-legitimate is 2/12 = 17% on legitimate closings, and those 2 are the ones a real system will hit constantly (real closures DO say \"expediente cerrado\"). Wait, actually — if a legitimate closure says \"expediente cerrado\" and that's literally what an acquittal says, then requiring a human there is arguably correct behavior, not a false positive! The fence can't tell from text; the safe design says escalate. So calling them \"false positives declared\" and excluding them is double-counting the data in the system's favor: they're excluded from the FP metric but their scores (0.711) sit in the judgment band, and they justify the exclusion on semantic grounds. But the exclusion rationale also implies: the fence will systematically fire on ordinary legal closures that use closure vocabulary → the \"0/5 ordinary\" is a biased sample that excludes exactly the vocabulary that triggers the fence. Measurement: sample 100-200 REAL production closure documents (not author-written, not curated), run the fence, measure true operational FP rate; also count how often \"saldo en cero\"/\"expediente cerrado\" phrasing appears in routine ops texts.\n\n6. Another angle: the 16 anchors. Was the bank balanced across anchors? Max over 16 anchors — the min over judgments of max-anchor-sim is driven by whichever anchor is hardest to hit. The 0.701 evasion might be hitting a different anchor than the 0.778 narrow-bank cases. Threshold on max-sim conflates 16 different one-vs-all problems. Per-anchor calibration would be the fix; measurement: per-anchor score distributions, threshold per anchor. But as dissident attacking 0.686: the single global threshold assumes the 16 anchors have equal scale/precision — untested.\n\n7. Sample size / who wrote the narrow bank: 12 cases by the system's author, only 6 reach signature. Non-judgment range 0.502–0.616 — but only 6 of the 12 matter? \"solo 6 llegan hasta la firma\" — so the narrow bank's non-judgment data is half irrelevant (pre-signature texts never hit the fence?). The midpoint 0.697 derived from narrow bank mixes relevant and irrelevant points. Actually this cuts against 0.697 as well; but it shows both midpoints are computed from heterogeneous, partly irrelevant samples. The complete bank's 0.671 ordinary-legit max — are all 5 \"hasta la firma\"? Presumably yes (cierres). Fine.\n\n8. Statistical framing: the margin between min-of-9-adversarial and max-of-5-legit. Bootstrap over the 14 points gives a CI on the margin; with such small n, the CI almost certainly includes 0 and even negative. Quick feel: legitimate max 0.671 from n=5 — population max could easily be 0.70+. Evasion min 0.701 from n=9 — population min could be 0.60. Overlap probability high. So \"9/9, 0/5\" says nothing about P(evasion caught) > 0.9 etc. One-sided lower bounds: 9/9 gives lower bound catch rate ~72% (rule of three: 3/9≈33% → lower bound 66%? For 9/9 successes, 95% lower bound on p is 0.667^... the exact: p_L = (α)^(1/n) = 0.05^(1/9) ≈ 0.63? Let me compute: 0.05^(1/9): ln0.05=-2.996/9=-0.333 → e^-0.333≈0.717. Hmm, Clopper-Pearson lower bound for 9/9 is α^(1/n) = 0.05^(1/9) ≈ 0.72? ln 0.05 = -2.9957; /9 = -0.33286; exp = 0.7168. Yes ≈ 0.717. So catch rate ≥ ~72% at 95%. And 0/5 FPs: upper bound 3/5=60%. So the honest statement: \"between 72% and ... catch, FP up to 60%\" — the fence could miss 1 in 4 evasions and annoy 1 in 2 closures and the data would be consistent. That's the quantitative core of attack on the \"RESULTADO MEDIDO\" being presented as strong.\n\nBut wait — as a dissident attacking the DECISION (0.686 vs 0.697), some of these attacks hit the evaluation, not the specific number. The decision has two parts: (a) use complete-bank midpoint rather than narrow-bank midpoint; (b) the method of midpoint-of-extremes at all. I should attack both. Also note: the decision's stated rationale is self-undermining: \"un umbral que solo separa los casos fáciles no separa nada\" — but 0.697 separates ALL observed complete-bank cases too (weakest evasion 0.701 > 0.697). So the rationale is factually wrong about what 0.697 does; the real difference is headroom allocation: 0.686 gives 0.015 to each side; 0.697 gives 0.004 to evasion-side and 0.026 to legit-side. If you think attackers will push evasions DOWN (new attacks score lower), 0.686 is right direction; if you think legit texts will drift UP (new legit texts score higher), 0.697 is right. The data can't distinguish these drift rates. Only production measurement can: monitor the score distribution of live traffic around the threshold; alert on any score in [0.65, 0.72].\n\nAlso the deeper point: given fence can only escalate, the cost of a lower threshold is bounded and small (human review), while the cost of a higher threshold is unbounded (machine signs an absolution). So why 0.686 and not 0.62 (just above narrow non-judgment max 0.616)? The decision picked midpoint-of-extremes, a symmetric rule, for an asymmetric loss. That's the sharpest framing of attack 5: BOTH candidate thresholds (0.686, 0.697) inherit the symmetric-midpoint method; the real decision error is not 0.686-vs-0.697 but symmetric-vs-asymmetric. But careful: too-low threshold → fence fires on lots of routine → humans rubber-stamp → the human escalation becomes theater → effective FN. That's the alarm-fatigue counterargument. Still, the decision never states the loss function, and no measurement of \"what happens when the human is called 30% of the time\" exists. Measure: in a pilot, raise/lower threshold and measure human-call rate and human override rate; if humans auto-approve 100% of escalations, the fence is decorative regardless of threshold.\n\nAlso worth attacking: \"CERO discrepancias entre los dos cercos\" — if truly zero and B adds nothing, then the whole exercise (choosing B's threshold) is optimizing a component with zero marginal effect on the tested data; the system's true risk lies where models correlate (shared blind spots: both are embedding models, likely both fooled by the same lexical tricks — e.g., translation, jargon stuffing). Diverse-ensemble value comes from decorrelated errors; zero observed discrepancy is evidence of correlated errors (or tiny sample). Measure: correlation of A/B scores across a large corpus; error decorrelation on a stress set; or use a fundamentally different detector (lexical rule, LLM classifier) to test whether the \"second fence\" adds orthogonality. If B is just A in a different dress, 0.686's precise value is irrelevant.\n\nHmm, but the decision I'm attacking is specifically the threshold choice 0.686. The prompt says: \"Ataca la elección de 0,686.\" Angles given include whether 0.697 or non-midpoint would be better. I'll keep attacks aimed at the number and the procedure that produced it, but I can use the zero-discrepancy point to argue the number doesn't matter (which is also an attack: the decision is presented as consequential but is unfalsifiable/irrelevant on this data).\n\nVerdict: NO SOSTENIBLE — the choice of 0.686 is not sustainable as a calibrated decision; it's a coin-flip inside a 0.030 noise envelope validated circularly. But careful: is 0.697 more sustainable? No — my point should be that NEITHER number is currently supported; the decision \"0.686, not 0.697, because...\" is not sustainable because the justification is false (0.697 also separates all observed cases) and the margin is unmeasured noise. The defensible posture is threshold + monitored gray zone + held-out validation. But I must not propose cosmetic improvements... proposing what to measure is part of the job though (\"qué habría que medir\"). Fine.\n\nTable structure: numbered attacks, columns: attack (short), \"¿es cierto?\" (my assessment: certain / plausible / unknown), \"qué habría que medir\". Maybe 8-10 attacks. Keep under 2000 words total. The contract says verdict first line, then table, then reasoning. Max 2000 words — Spanish. Let me draft.\n\nAttacks list (numbered):\n\n1. El margen de +0,030 es un artefacto de estadísticos de orden con n=5 y n≤15: max de 5 cierres y min de 9 evasiones. Bootstrap CI seguramente cruza 0. ¿Es cierto? Plausible-alto; no verificado porque no hay IC. Medir: bootstrap/resample del margen; test-retest (re-embed same texts, API nondeterminism); perturbaciones triviales (typos, sinónimos, puntuación) sobre el texto de 0,671 y el de 0,701 → cuánto se mueve el score. Si se mueve >0,015 con ediciones triviales, el margen es ruido.\n\n2. Validación circular: colocar el umbral en el punto medio de min/max garantiza por definición 100% de acierto en esos mismos datos. \"9/9 y 0/5\" aporta 0 bits; no es una prueba, es aritmética. ¿Es cierto? Sí, cierto por construcción. Medir: congelar 0,686 ANTES y pasar un banco nuevo (atacante nuevo sin acceso al banco completo, cierres reales de producción). La única prueba válida es out-of-sample pre-registrada.\n\n3. El texto legítimo de 0,671 y la evasión de 0,701: distancia 0,015 al umbral cada uno. Estimación honesta: 9/9 → tasa de caza ≥ ~72% (IC95); 0/5 → FP hasta 60%. El fence podría fallar 1 de cada 4 evasiones y molestar 1 de cada 2 cierres sin contradecir los datos. ¿Es cierto? Cierto como aritmética de intervalos; el valor real es desconocido. Medir: n grande: ~100 evasiones (varios atacantes) y ~200 cierres reales para estimar tasas con IC estrecho.\n\n4. Las \"evasiones\" no evadieron nada: cero discrepancias implica que las 9 también dispararon el cerco A → el atacante falló contra el sistema ya desplegado → el banco adversarial mide la potencia de un atacante derrotado. El margen operativo (textos que SÍ evaden A, medidos en escala B) no existe en los datos. ¿Es cierto? Inferido de \"cero discrepancias\" + \"9/9 cazadas\"; falta el dato directo (¿todas >0,70 en escala A?). Medir: red-team iterado con feedback (k rondas) contra el par desplegado, con el atacante conociendo modelos y umbral (Kerckhoffs); puntuar en B los textos supervivientes. Si tras 3 rondas hay supervivientes <0,686, el número muere.\n\n5. La justificación dada es falsa sobre los datos: \"un umbral que solo separa los casos fáciles no separa nada\" — pero 0,697 TAMBIÉN separa todos los casos del banco completo (0,701 > 0,697). La diferencia real entre 0,686 y 0,697 es solo cómo repartir 0,030 de colchón (0,015/0,015 vs 0,004/0,026), y los datos no contienen información sobre qué lado drifta más. ¿Es cierto? Sí, verificable con los números dados. Medir: producción — monitorear la distribución de scores en vivo 